text:
maria: Quick report: intermittent failures in our browser + node SDKs when SSE payloads are split mid-JSON. Repro'd in the node-fetch sample app and browser integration tests. Logs below.

sam: Do we have an example of the split?

maria: Yep. Server emitted a single JSON event but the TCP stream split it: 

chunk A: "data: {\"id\":42, \"msg\": \"Sta\"\n"
chunk B: "rted\"}\n
Our naive client logic was: accumulator += chunk; if (accumulator.includes('\n\n')) parse each event; which throws `Unexpected end of JSON input` when split happens inside a string.

josh: Could be proxy buffering behavior (nginx/Envoy) or just small writes from the runtime. HTTP/2 frames also change how bytes arrive.

eva: I added a harness that writes varying chunk sizes and reproduced reliably by forcing small write sizes. Observed two bad outcomes: 1) parser throws and triggers immediate reconnect, causing duplicated events on retry; 2) client waits until overall read timeout (30s) because it expects \n\n boundary.

sam: Proposed fixes: server side: emit each SSE data block as a single write and add keepalive comments; client side: incremental parsing so partial JSON doesn't throw and we don't immediately reconnect. Also dedupe events using the `id:` field on reconnect.

maria: Agreed. Short-term pragmatic steps: 1) add streaming-json fallback in SDKs; 2) default keepalive every 10s via `: keepalive\n\n`; 3) expose SDK options `sseKeepaliveInterval` and `sseRetryDebounceMs`; 4) add nginx recommendations like `proxy_buffering off; proxy_request_buffering off; sendfile off;`.

josh: I tested nginx with those flags in staging and it dropped failures from ~8/min to 0 over a 30m stress test. Will run longer.

eva: Tests needed: unit test simulating partial chunk sequences ensuring only one final JSON object is emitted and no throw; e2e stress harness with and without proxy.

sam: Implementing in SDK: incremental buffer, decode with {stream:true} if available, split on \n\n but keep trailing partial in buffer, and only JSON.parse when the data: line is complete. Also add a debounce that waits 200ms for more bytes before treating a parse as fatal.

maria: Docs PR will include server examples and code snippet. Example robust browser reader I plan to include: 

```js
let buf = '';
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
fetch(url).then(r => {
  const reader = r.body.getReader();
  function readChunk() {
    return reader.read().then(({done, value}) => {
      if (done) return;
      buf += decoder.decode(value, {stream: true});
      const events = buf.split('\n\n');
      buf = events.pop(); // keep partial
      for (const ev of events) {
        const lines = ev.split('\n').filter(Boolean);
        const dataLine = lines.find(l => l.startsWith('data:'));
        if (!dataLine) continue;
        const jsonText = dataLine.replace(/^data:\s*/, '');
        try { handle(JSON.parse(jsonText)); }
        catch (e) { /* swallow: wait for next chunk to complete the JSON */ }
      }
      return readChunk();
    });
  }
  readChunk();
});
```

eva: We'll add unit tests that feed partial buffers to the decoder and ensure no exception and a single delivered payload.

sam: Also adding dedupe by tracking last-seen `id` per connection and ignoring duplicates on reconnect within a short window.

ci-bot: maria opened branch devex/sse-parser-fallback and pushed 0004-streaming-parser.js (CI running)

josh: After CI passes, roll to staging and run Eva's harness for 2 hours. If green, plan a prod config roll and SDK patch release.

maria: Action owners: maria (docs + examples + tests), sam (SDK streaming parser + options + dedupe), josh (proxy config + staging run), eva (harness + extended tests). Tracking on #devex-sse. Thanks everyone :thumbsup:
